2026-04-30 07:18:09,445 [nexau.archs.main_sub.execution.tool_executor] INFO: 🔧 Executing tool 'write_file' for agent 'agent_c62eface' with parameters: {'file_path': '/inspire/qb-ilm/project/cq-scientific-cooperation-zone/public/ywang/meta-harness-evolver/project/test/project-bda/hoss-evolution-workspaces/Carnevale22_Adenosine/run-2/candidates/candidate_5/proposer_reasoning.md', 'content': '# Evolution Proposal - Candidate 5\n\n## What Changed\n\nReplaced the **UCB (Upper Confidence Bound)** algorithm with **Thompson Sampling** using a Beta-Bernoulli model. This is a fundamentally different approach to the exploration-exploitation tradeoff:\n\n1. **Probabilistic Sampling**: Instead of computing deterministic confidence bounds, Thompson Sampling samples from posterior distributions\n2. **Beta-Bernoulli Model**: Models the hit probability for each gene using a Beta distribution\n3. **Bayesian Prior**: Uses gene family statistics to create informative priors (empirical Bayes)\n4. **Natural Exploration**: Exploration emerges naturally from posterior uncertainty rather than being explicitly added\n\n## Why This Change\n\n**Theoretical Foundation**: Thompson Sampling is proven to be asymptotically optimal for bandit problems and often outperforms UCB in practice because it:\n- Naturally balances exploration and exploitation without tuning exploration constants\n- Provides better uncertainty quantification through full posterior distributions\n- Adapts more gracefully to the actual reward distribution\n\n**Biological Relevance**: The Beta-Bernoulli model is well-suited for hit/no-hit binary outcomes:\n- Successes (hits) and failures (non-hits) directly update the Beta posterior\n- Gene family priors allow sharing information across related genes\n- The probabilistic framework handles uncertainty in hit predictions more naturally\n\n**Explore Mode Requirement**: This is a scheduled exploration round requiring a "meaningfully different approach." Thompson Sampling represents a different algorithmic family (Bayesian sampling vs. optimism-in-the-face-of-uncertainty).\n\n## Expected Impact\n\n**Hypothesis**: Thompson Sampling will:\n1. **Improve early-round performance** by better uncertainty handling\n2. **Reduce hyperparameter sensitivity** (no exploration constant to tune)\n3. **Better exploit family patterns** through empirical Bayes priors\n4. **Achieve competitive or better scores** than the current best (29.41)\n\n**Conservative Design**: \n- Prior strength = 5 (equivalent to 5 pseudo-observations)\n- Family-based priors default to global hit rate\n- Maintains stratified diversity for first round\n\n## Novelty\n\n### Strategy Tags\n- **bayesian**: Uses Bayesian inference with posterior sampling\n- **probabilistic**: Samples from probability distributions rather than deterministic scoring\n\n### Comparison to Recent Candidates\n\n**Most similar to**: Candidate 4 (both use gene family information)\n\n**Key differences**:\n1. **Algorithmic paradigm**: Thompson Sampling (Bayesian sampling) vs. UCB (frequentist confidence bounds)\n2. **Exploration mechanism**: Natural posterior uncertainty vs. explicit exploration bonus\n3. **Family integration**: Empirical Bayes priors vs. additive family bonus\n4. **Hyperparameters**: Prior strength vs. exploration constant and family weight\n\n**Why this is meaningfully different**:\n- UCB explicitly adds an exploration term (√(2.5·log(t)/n)) to encourage trying uncertain arms\n- Thompson Sampling samples from the posterior and naturally explores arms with high uncertainty\n- This leads to different selection dynamics and potentially better performance, especially with limited data\n\n## Technical Details\n\nThe algorithm works as follows:\n1. **Track hits/failures**: Count successes (hits=1) and failures (hits=0) for each gene\n2. **Family statistics**: Calculate empirical hit rates per gene family\n3. **Bayesian prior**: Use family hit rates as informative priors (strength=5)\n4. **Posterior sampling**: Sample hit probability from Beta(α, β) where:\n   - α = successes + prior_successes + 1\n   - β = failures + prior_failures + 1\n5. **Select top samples**: Choose genes with highest sampled probabilities\n\nThis approach maintains the biological insight from candidate 4 (family patterns) while adopting a theoretically principled Bayesian framework.'}
